            <ArticleTitle>Patient, home residence, and neighborhood characteristics in pediatric emergency department visits for asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>95-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To identify patient, home residence, and neighborhood characteristics of children with asthma-related emergency department visits.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Medical records of children with one (group A) or more than one (group B) asthma-related pediatric emergency department visit were reviewed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A significantly higher percentage of group B had Medicaid insurance (p = 0.04), history of asthma-related hospitalizations (p = 0.04), and passive tobacco smoke exposure (p = 0.03). Neighborhood characteristics were similar between the two groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Smoking cessation counseling and close monitoring of patients with a history of asthma-related hospitalizations and patients with Medicaid insurance may be helpful in decreasing emergency department visits.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
